it's great to welcome to the program  today Roger Hallam who's a British  environmental activist and co-founder of  extinction rebellion a movement that  uses nonviolent civil disobedience to  compel government action on climate  change  Roger it's so great to have you on I  appreciate it yeah great friends so this  is a topic I'm very interested in  because I've long argued that here in  the United States for example calling  your elected official and signing a  petition online is very rarely going to  really achieve the kind of systemic  change that we need in order to deal  with some of the major issues that are  facing us climate change and  environmental issues certainly included  and I know that for me violent civil  disobedience feels like it is over the  line but finding exactly where the line  is is maybe more complicated than some  might think so can you talk a little bit  about your perspective in some of the  protests you've organized well the first  thing to say is as I'm sure people know  and the climate change crisis has been  going on now for 30 years since 1990  when scientists first told people they  were heading for this massive  catastrophe if something isn't doesn't  happen so as you write you say people  been sending emails and lobbying and and  doing all the conventional stuff for 30  years and carbon emissions have gone up  by 60% which is beyond bad as as  everyone knows so you know extinction  rebellion came along will you with a  black sheet of paper a bit like what you  said and said okay so that's not working  so what needs to work and the fact of  the matter is is civil disobedience does  work as long as it's constructed in a  particular way and just to sort of  expand on that civil disobedience is a  way of peacefully breaking the law they  involves respect towards your opponent  so it's really a little bit complicated  to get your head round because obviously  you're disrupting people's lives and the  government but the same time you're  saying look we want to talk and we want  to sort this problem out and that's  really wasting some rebelling is about  and what civil disappearance is about  can you talk a little bit about some  the tactics and techniques that that you  use yeah the main the main way of  disrupting governments I guess is to sit  in a road some people paint building  some people go on hunger strike none of  this is what people want to do I hasten  to add right it's not the sort of thing  you take do lightly but thousands of  people around the world have now made a  decision because the pennies drop that  nothing else is working and that's the  thing that gets the disruption those two  things going on you're disrupting people  but also people are impressed by your  sacrifice people work you know getting  arrested going to prison and  what-have-you and obviously no one does  that lightly so people don't tend to  think even if they disagree with you it  I think at least a go well you know the  guy is actually standing by way believes  and so maybe there's something to what  he's saying so that's really how it  works  do we have data as to what the public  perception is of some of these actions  because for example in the United States  we've had a number of different protests  that involved shutting down a bridge  which is a key commuting artery into or  out of a city and at least anecdotally a  lot of the reaction seems to be I need  to go to work and if I'm not there I get  fired I can no longer feed my kids and  it's it's in a sense of violent act  against my ability to put food on my  family's table so I wonder what the  reaction do we have anything empirical  about how people do react when they hear  about such a protest that is it for  example a blocking the road protest well  the first thing to say is you know it's  very no-brainer  obviously it's totally maddening if  someone's stopping you getting to work  so no one's trying to dispute that the  point of civil disobedience is you're  taking responsibility you're not trying  to run away from your actions it's not  like you're blocking the road and then  running off you're basically saying to  people I'm blocking a road I know I'm  acting illegally and I'm more prepared  I'm prepared to go to court and you know  suffer for why I've done I'm prepared to  get fined I'm prepared to go to prison  if needs be so that changes things a  little bit but in terms of how it  the public like what we need to look at  is like the public as a whole and I can  give you an interesting stat for  instance but what happened last year if  you want some specific data on it like  most people in Britain hadn't really  heard of the climate emergency a bit  like in America everyone thought it was  a little bit of a small problem but  actually it's a massive problem and  1,200 people were arrested last April it  was the biggest civil disobedience in  British history  and after that the episode 67% of the  British population agreed there was a  climbing emergency in other words I was  like tens of millions of conversations  in pubs you know around kitchen tables  where people were going and going a  minute  there's thousands people getting  arrested this is a big issue we sort of  knew it was a big issue and now we know  it's a big issue and that's how attitude  change works so in other words even if  at the micro level someone is prevented  from getting to work as an example if  the social consciousness rises to the  point where you have critical mass  towards action you you consider it a net  benefit that's that's what happens on a  social level yeah but it has to be done  in a respectful way and this is the  thing that people often forget this  isn't like just without rioting or about  being sort of aggressive or what have  you it's calm is order disrespectful and  then as I said even if people disagree  with you they certainly have a you know  a certain respect for you putting  yourself on the line and I think the  other thing we need to understand is  that civil just being it's worked but it  only works if you're doing something on  an issue that's right you know if I sit  in a road and block you getting to work  and I want you to wear a red hat for me  or something you're gonna say I don't  care that you're sitting in the road you  know no way am I going to do that for  you it only really works when everyone  knows it's actually real you know this  is objectively real what's happening  with the climate crisis and this is the  biggest problem is communicating that  we're absolutely in this dire situation  and that's why people are taking this  you know dramatic step one of the  tactics that seems to be more of a gray  area for differ  activists depending on where they draw  the line is the destruction of for  example imagine that you're protesting  logging going in the middle of the night  so nobody gets hurt and disabling or  damaging bulldozers as an example its  destruction of property certainly it's  certainly a crime but no one is being  physically hurt where is something like  that in your sort of paradigm well to be  honest with you like the old sort of way  of doing direct action as far as I'm  concerned and I've done five years of  research at King's College in London on  it the main way to change society is  through mass participation civil  disobedience which means like tens of  thousands even hundreds of thousands of  people breaking the law in a small way  in urban centres where everyone can see  them where that's where the government  is was the media and what-have-you in  other words you go to the capital city  and this sort of thing has been  happening in in many countries of the  world for the last 50 years particularly  the global south well you know you've  probably seen a psychic in Algeria or  Sudan or many other countries people go  to the capital city and say we don't  want to live under a dictatorship under  corruption anymore and we're sitting  down until we get some semblance of  democracy so we're really we're  borrowing that methodology from those  people and applying it twisting  democracies where we have this corrupt  situation of governments listening to  fossil fuel companies a lot more to  ordinary citizens who are concerned  about their children's future so that's  really what we're aiming for rather than  you know 10 people doing something in  the middle of the night as it were so in  other words it's less about figuring out  whether destruction of property is on  this side or that side of the line but  recognizing that that's too small in  action compared to the mass rally at an  urban centre to really achieve the sort  of attention that you believe is  necessary to get change to happen  yeah and it's not to be honest with you  primarily about the physical disruption  it's more about the emotional  communication you know when you see  someone who's 84 or a kid who's 14  getting dragged off by the police  they're not enough it immediately has a  emotional reaction you know whether you  opposed to it or not you're thinking  what's going on  you know and when normal people come out  on the streets and this is what it's all  about it's not about activists or  whatever you know I'm sure I'm a farmer  I've been doing growing food for people  for thirty years now and it's normal  people that are starting to take the  initiative here rather than what you  might call the old activist communities  and that's the difference with  extinction rebellion is it's bringing up  people from lots of different politics  and cultural backgrounds you know from  small business people you know single  mothers you know people from all  different backgrounds to saying hang on  a minute this is a universal issue which  affects everything that I believe in you  know whether you've got right-wing  values or left-wing values right no one  wants to see the destruction of America  right is that right I think so for the  most part I think that's true yeah and  that's why I'm coming to America to do  you know I'm promoting my book common  sense it's called common sense because  it's related to Thomas Paine I don't  know you know Thomas Paine because  America in the revolution you said to  Americans you know you're not going to  get rid of the British unless you just  have a revolution and say no we don't  want the British crown anymore and what  I'm saying to the American public is you  need to deal with this directly by  having thousands of people on the street  saying we don't actually want the  destruction of our families or  communities and the American nation  that's fundamentally what what this is  about no question and we've been talking  about the importance of mass rallies at  urban centers as a tool for achieving  change for years on the program Roger  Hallam is a British environmental  activist co-founder of extinction  rebellion author of common sense  Rogers such a pleasure having you on and  I appreciate you sharing your  perspective yeah well thank you good  luck

